"""layers.0.mlp.down_proj:862 -- a sparse, mostly lexical component with no clean concept.

What it does: its activation is a signed coefficient on a fixed output direction. It is
mostly set by WHICH token is being read (a per-token table explains ~45% of its variance on
held-out text, the top 3000 tokens ~20%), but the signs are spread over tens of thousands of
word pieces with no shared category. Category means over script, part of speech, or word
length are all ~0. Only a few small word groups have a consistent sign, and they are the only
concepts this program uses:

  - chat / reasoning markup tags (<think>, </think>, <tool_response>, ...): clearly negative
  - code-project words (/test, README, param, .Printf, static, Setter, ...): mildly positive
  - modal and instruction words (should, may, can, keep, make, following, ...): mildly positive
  - quantity and degree words (most, several, long, example, its, about, ...): mildly negative
  - CJK characters: slightly positive

Every other token predicts 0. The values are the group means on training text. On held-out
text this explains under 1% of the variance, so the program is an honest description of the
few consistent signs, not a faithful reconstruction of the component.

Main program: `step` looks up the newest token's string and returns its group's mean.
"""

CHAT_TAGS = {"<think>", "</think>", "<tool_response>", "</tool_response>"}
CODE_WORDS = {
    "/test", "/tests", "readme", "param", ".printf", "setter", "static", ".junit",
    ".vaadin", "/java", "/src", ".go", ".google", ".springframework", ".ts", "-term", "__",
}
MODAL_WORDS = {
    "should", "may", "can", "could", "might", "must", "will", "keep", "make",
    "following", "your", "need", "want", "use",
}
QUANTITY_WORDS = {
    "long", "most", "more", "several", "multiple", "many", "few", "example", "format",
    "its", "their", "those", "about", "very", "less", "some", "various", "certain",
    "available", "close", "loss", "baby", "series", "us", "form", "often", "usually",
}

READS = ()


def _is_cjk(text):
    return any("一" <= ch <= "鿿" for ch in text)


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    text = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    word = text.strip().lower()
    if text.strip() in CHAT_TAGS:
        value = -0.07
    elif word in CODE_WORDS:
        value = 0.035
    elif word in MODAL_WORDS:
        value = 0.016
    elif word in QUANTITY_WORDS:
        value = -0.026
    elif _is_cjk(text):
        value = 0.006
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return carry, float(value)

